      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find \\(\\cos 135^\\circ\\), we can use the angle subtraction formula for cosine, which states that \\(\\cos(a - b) = \\cos a \\cos b + \\sin a \\sin b\\). We can express \\(135^\\circ\\) as \\(180^\\circ - 45^\\circ\\), so we have:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 135^\\circ = \\cos(180^\\circ - 45^\\circ) = \\cos 180^\\circ \\cos 45^\\circ + \\sin 180^\\circ \\sin 45^\\circ\n\\]\n\nWe know the values of the trigonometric functions at these angles:\n\\[\n\\cos 180^\\circ = -1, \\quad \\cos 45^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\quad \\sin 180^\\circ = 0, \\quad \\sin 45^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these values into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\cos 135^\\circ = (-1) \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) + (0) \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) = -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\cos 135^\\circ\\) is \\(\\boxed{-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}\\).",
